Decode profile paths as UTF-8 when handing them to std::filesystem

Chkdraft keeps file system paths in std::string as UTF-8, and most of its
file handling says so: std::filesystem::path(asUtf8(...)) to build a path,
u8string() to read one back (map_file.cpp, system_io.cpp). The profile code
passes the plain std::string instead. libstdc++ decodes a narrow path as
UTF-8, but libc++ decodes it in the ANSI code page, so on clang64 a non-ASCII
character anywhere in the settings folder's path points Chkdraft at a folder
that does not exist, and it terminates while loading profiles at start-up:

    libc++abi: terminating due to uncaught exception of type
    std::__1::__fs::filesystem::filesystem_error: filesystem error: in
    directory_iterator::directory_iterator(...): The system cannot find the
    path specified.

While the folder sat beside the executable, that took an MSYS2 installation
under a non-ASCII path. Under %LOCALAPPDATA% (0009) a user name with a
non-ASCII letter in it is enough. Build these paths with asUtf8 and read them
back with u8string(): listing, comparing, creating, renaming and removing
profiles, changing into a profile's folder, and making a custom data file path
relative to its profile.

Upstream: not submitted yet
Applies-to: all environments (the failure shows on clang64)

